# CTXD and Maestro project contract

Use the project binding in .cursor/project-governance.json and the project rule
in .cursor/rules/10-project-settings.mdc. Confirm the repository, CTXD project,
Maestro workspace and project against live records. A binding is routing context.
Preserve applicable project instructions and approved product and design decisions.
Follow the established project-specific workflow and canonical CTXD runbook.

CTXD holds durable project context and decisions. Maestro holds scope, tasks,
ownership, dependencies and acceptance evidence. Use a verified Maestro task
for implementation and preserve other workers' branches and worktrees. Keep
project procedures exclusively in CTXD RUNBOOKS.MD. Repository rules can point
to procedures and define role boundaries.

A delegated worker uses its supplied assignment, identity and context.
It stays in the assigned worktree and returns evidence to its controller.
Tracker mutations, new credentials and broader tool access are outside that
worker role. A direct Cursor session follows the project's own access
and approval policy.

Require the project's applicable tests, CI, browser or physical validation
and checkable evidence for task closure. Source integration and production
acceptance are separate milestones. An agent's summary is not acceptance evidence.

Keep independent review separate from implementation. Automated reviewers use
the project's isolated read-only wrapper and exact PR commits. Only the trusted
outer wrapper publishes its verdict. Use the existing separate approver where
the project has one. Human-only review or merge policies still apply. Only
Joshua merges main. Promotion triggers and release procedures live in the
project's canonical CTXD runbook and release policy. Respect protected branches.

These rules apply to authorized work within the current session. Use installed
orchestration tools where applicable and preserve work and evidence on handoff.
